'cand_46044a8d593f57078c8fad1bfaacefe3':'The complete soft-elbow overhead oval with separated fingertips and connected shoulders is ordinary ballet geometry but would free both hands from the bowl and make the person demonstrate a ritual-looking pose. At creativity=1 that pose shifts attention from the practical return to performance; no overhead-arm effect retained.',
'cand_4d90460656185e95ac735aa6d90a563f':'Clamshell requires upper dominant frontal soft key, weaker lower frontal return, vertical high/low catchlights, lifted yet present eye/nose/chin shadows and dimensional modeling together. Its declared effects are empty and the setup would erase the motivated warm hearth/blue door separation. No source lighting contract retained.',
'cand_8fb45cf54296562a8686d81030fc85ac':'The full near-ninety-degree face key requires a face-center nose boundary, distinct bright/shadow halves, preserved shaded eye/cheek/jaw and modeled planes. It is not proved by generic hearth-side illumination; no explicit writable effects or source admission are supplied. No split-light claim retained.',
'cand_ded03c8f06af5522aac34cf1546c8fee':'Intentional eye/nose-zone occlusion requires a scene-bound overlap or head turn and a readable adult unfinished action/setting. Deliberately hiding both eyes and nose would undermine the chosen reference-appearance use; its source has no declared writable effects. No occlusion effect retained.',
'cand_e9eac216926b54258dd791f3feaf08df':'Folk R02 requires a distinct host offering a filled bowl, guest, and another adult across the open exit behind that guest. The baseline has a returning carrier and two distant witnesses, not those three relational owners. Creating a blocking host/guest arrangement adds subject/count/age effects outside open scope and changes the local return into a hospitality trap. No admitted alternative or retained exit-blocking claim.'}
